    {
      "contact": "bob",
      "content": "Bob's file predates the CRM and arrived via the old CSV import, which means every field was flattened and then re-expanded — so this note carries the history of that migration: the original row had three employers, two addresses, and a phone number written five different ways across five years of exports, and the import merge reconciled them into the current record. The import tool wrote its audit trail here: it kept the first employer as primary because the second address stopped being valid after the 2018 move; it resolved the phone-number country-code discrepancy in favour of the +49 value over the 0049 spelling because the latter only ever appeared in the old export format; and it merged three email addresses by de-duplicating the two that differed only by a trailing dot. This note is deliberately long because long notes are a separate failure class: a text column that is fine at 200 characters can silently truncate, mangle, or fail to round-trip at 2000, and this contact is the regression net for exactly that. The length also exercises the FTS tokenizer against a single large field rather than many small ones, which is a different query plan. Past the two-thousand-character mark the content itself no longer matters; what matters is that every character — the umlauts, the em dashes, the digits, the trailing punctuation, and the repeated long words that a truncating implementation would cut in half — survives a save, a migration, an export, and a re-import byte for byte. A truncation would also be visible in the raw byte count, which is why the suite that consumes this fixture checks the length of this exact field rather than trusting the round trip alone.",
      "date": "2026-02-01T12:00:00Z"
    },
